WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:06.269
Protests started in Hong Kong in late September.
What had happened is that the Chinese government

00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:10.769
had come out and said 'hey we know we promised
your first fully-democratic election in 2017,

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:16.129
but we're going to approve any of the candidates
who can run.' People started protesting and

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:19.710
then few days later, the thing that really
escalated it was when Hong Kong police came

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:24.630
out with an unprecedented level of force to
crack down on the protesters, which only further

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.230
outraged a lot of people in Hong Kong, drove
more people into the streets where they're

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.540
now shutting down the financial district.
To understand what's really happening here,

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.429
the larger forces at work, you have to go
back to 1997. That's the year of what people

00:00:37.429 --> 00:00:41.679
in Hong Kong call the handover, when it went
from one of the last vestiges of the British

00:00:41.679 --> 00:00:46.739
Empire to part of China. As part of the handover,
China promised that Hong Kong would be able

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:52.539
to keep this really unusual level of freedom,
and it would get to have these first fully-democratic

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:58.069
elections in 2017. It's called the One Country,
Two Systems policy. So when China reneged

00:00:58.069 --> 00:01:02.829
a little bit on its plan for the 2017 elections,
it looked to a lot of people in Hong Kong

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:06.850
like it was the beginning of the end of One
Country, Two Systems. And the thing that you

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.740
have to understand is that it's about more
than just democracy, although that's important.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.810
There's a kind of sense of Hong Kong exceptionalism.
People there are much more affluent, much

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.250
more worldly. They really see themselves as
very distinct from the rest of China. And

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:27.060
any move like this that feels like it's pulling
them into the dictatorial rule of the communist

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.020
party in China is very scary to them. And
part of what really makes that so scary is

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.070
the memory of Tiananmen Square, right, which
happened in 1989 in Beijing. That memory is

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.560
much, much stronger in Hong Kong, where they
hold an annual vigil every year. They're keeping

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.380
the flame alive for their fellow Chinese who
can't, so that when police started coming

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.509
out in force late in these protests, they
thought 'hey this doesn't look like Hong Kong,

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:53.490
this looks like Beijing.' So what you're seeing
is not just a fight for, can we keep the election

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.670
rules that we have for 2017, it's a fight
for, can they stay Hong Kong or are they going

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.009
to become just like the rest of China?

